# RT100 screen project — state and next tasks

## State (working, verified on the device)
- Screen runs custom firmware `device/custom/out/firmwareOledFile.bin` (I2C clock fix +
  custom display mode). Do not rebuild/reflash it for host-side work.
- `screen/rt100screen.py run` rotates clock, system, now-playing, weather, next meeting
  and volume. A desktop notification (app + summary only) replaces the page for 10 s.
  `--no-notify` disables that. Battery is on the system page. A timer is started from
  the control page.
- Next meeting is the Teams calendar (`screen/teamscal.py`), not the Google calendars
  in GNOME. Sign-in opens a browser as Microsoft Graph Command Line Tools; the GNOME
  app cannot read calendars. The token lives in `~/.config/rt100screen-teams.json`
  (mode 0600). Do not print it, and do not commit it.
- Weather reads `~/.config/rt100screen.json`. It is set to London (51.513, -0.092).
  Change it only when the user asks: `screen/rt100screen.py location CITY`.
- Agent alerts: Cursor `stop` and Claude `Stop`/`Notification` write
  `~/.local/state/rt100screen/agent.json`. The screen holds that page for 3 minutes
  and flashes WAITING or NEEDS YOU. The app name and the window title are on the page.
  The next prompt clears it. Do not remove those hooks unless the user asks.
- Only one sender. A second copy exits. `screen/rt100screen.py stop` asks the running one to
  return the screen to the stock UI.
- Unplugging the wired keyboard leaves the last custom frame on the screen. The keyboard
  restarts its old interface when the cable returns, so the service clears the panel
  again at 2s and 5s. A purpose-drawn offline card needs a firmware change and an
  explicit yes before building it.
- The 2.4 GHz dongle (PID 0x4011, `--dongle`) is still untested. Do not use it unless the
  user explicitly says to try wireless.
- Dropped pictures are 64px or 120px. GIFs play up to 24 frames. `screen/rt100screen.py image`
  still scales to 64px; `--full` allows larger and is slow.
- `screen/rt100.py` = vendor HID client, `screen/screenproto.py` = packet encoder, `screen/oled_flash.py` = flasher (forbidden, see safety rule).
- Layout: `screen/` is the laptop program, `device/` is firmware, `research/` is the dumps and the vendor driver.

## Not done yet
- **Pacing** is still the guess of 6 µs per pixel. Change `--us-per-pixel` only while the
  user is watching a full-screen fill, and only lower it if the picture stays correct.

Autostart is enabled (`systemctl --user enable` already done). Do not disable it unless the user asks.

## Known open issues (report, don't fix blindly)
- Screen may turn off after ~2 min (keyboard sleep command `0x3F`); not yet seen in custom mode.
  If the user reports it, investigate and propose — any fix in firmware needs approval.
- Orientation is verified for text; check images the right way up with the user.

## Escalate to the user (stop and ask) when
- A task seems to need a firmware change, a new vendor command, or flashing.
- The device behaves unexpectedly, or a tool reports an error you don't understand.
